WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:02.250
It's just something
we don't like to think about.

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.500
No-one talks about it,
no-one really wants to address it.

00:00:04.583 --> 00:00:07.625
My husband's very much: "Don't want
to talk about it. Nope. You're fine."

00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:10.583
I don't want to talk about my death
too much with them

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:13.875
because I don't want to make them
feel more unhappy.

00:00:16.458 --> 00:00:18.625
It's even more difficult
if you're faced

00:00:18.708 --> 00:00:20.417
with somebody that you love

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.083
who's got a diagnosis

00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:24.833
and then you have to start
this conversation.

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:28.250
You don't have the language
because you’ve never talked about it.

00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:31.000
You don't have the familiarity, you
don't know how to open the question.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.375
In our society we're often encouraged

00:00:39.458 --> 00:00:40.500
to plan for our lives.

00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:42.167
Planning for our births,

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.875
planning for different operations
if they need to happen,

00:00:44.958 --> 00:00:46.542
and also, as an extension of that,

00:00:46.625 --> 00:00:48.750
starting to plan
for our end-of-life care.

00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:50.250
And that's increasingly important,

00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:53.542
because more of us have a period
where we are dying

00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:55.792
rather than just suddenly dead.

00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:58.708
So there's a chance to think about
what that period will look like.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:02.375
When those questions
are looking back at you

00:01:02.458 --> 00:01:05.208
and someone is saying,
"What do you want?"

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:09.333
It makes you really sit down
and think about

00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:13.292
all the places that you might want
to have your ashes scattered,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.792
or whether you want
to die in hospital,

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:18.000
or whether you want
to come home and die,

00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:20.500
what are you going
to do with your animals?

00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:23.333
Who's going to look after your
husband when you've gone?

00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:25.708
Families might make the assumption

00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:29.292
that they will be able to speak
on behalf of their loved one,

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:34.417
and it's not an automatic thing
that they can do that.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.417
It's important that they legally
nominate a family member

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.542
by making them a lasting power
of attorney for health and welfare.

00:01:46.208 --> 00:01:53.042
It's a comfort knowing that my
parents have power of attorney

00:01:53.125 --> 00:01:56.875
and they can make those decisions
when I'm not able to

00:01:56.958 --> 00:02:01.458
rather than a doctor or somebody
who doesn't know me very well.

00:02:01.792 --> 00:02:03.083
Just nudging doctors

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.708
in the right direction, so actually,

00:02:04.792 --> 00:02:06.417
"Do this, but don't do any more"

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.000
or, "This is not what I would have
wanted" is incredibly helpful.

00:02:10.292 --> 00:02:12.750
Legally we can't demand
certain treatments,

00:02:12.833 --> 00:02:14.417
but we can definitely
refuse treatments.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.958
So people can say they
don't want to be resuscitated,

00:02:17.042 --> 00:02:18.958
or they don't want
to have artificial nutrition.

00:02:19.042 --> 00:02:22.250
I think a funeral plan is extremely
helpful.

00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:25.125
Carl had made one, my husband,
before he died.

00:02:25.208 --> 00:02:28.542
So we did know what
he wanted in his funeral

00:02:28.625 --> 00:02:29.917
and what he didn't want.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.333
That kind of practical thing
was such a comfort to the family.

00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:37.875
Allow them to grieve for you,

00:02:38.208 --> 00:02:42.042
instead of having to worry
about the 'what ifs'.

00:02:42.125 --> 00:02:44.083
"What if we've done this wrong?"

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:49.958
We don't turn up and say:
"Let's talk about death."

00:02:50.042 --> 00:02:54.708
Or: "Tell me what you want
if I tell you that you're dying."

00:02:54.792 --> 00:02:57.625
You sent me your
Advance Care Plan booklet

00:02:57.708 --> 00:03:00.958
and I've filled that in
up to a point.

00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:02.500
Although when it came
to the, you know,

00:03:02.583 --> 00:03:04.833
"Do you want your window
open or shut? Or your light on?

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.333
Or what about your hygiene?"

00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:09.375
And I thought: "No - I can't
even think about that."

00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:13.792
But it is simply a case
of getting things together

00:03:13.875 --> 00:03:17.333
so that it's easy for my family.

00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:19.500
By documenting this information now

00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:23.292
you are at the centre and we,
as professionals, and your close ones

00:03:23.375 --> 00:03:29.042
and family, will do the best we can
to make sure you have the care

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:30.458
and the preferences you want.

00:03:30.875 --> 00:03:34.250
It's really important that you tell
your GP or your healthcare provider

00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:37.125
what your preferences are
if they relate to medical options.

00:03:37.208 --> 00:03:39.292
It's important to let your
friends and family know

00:03:39.375 --> 00:03:41.167
where they can find that document

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.042
if it's in a particular part
of your house.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.208
Disabled young people don't
necessarily want to be focusing

00:03:48.292 --> 00:03:49.708
on more planning

00:03:49.792 --> 00:03:51.125
which is around their illness

00:03:51.208 --> 00:03:52.667
or their disability.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:57.375
Personally for me I feel like
I've spent a lot of time

00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:02.000
negotiating my health, negotiating
the support that I receive,

00:04:02.083 --> 00:04:04.292
the care that I receive,
my diagnoses,

00:04:04.375 --> 00:04:10.458
and so I want to spend any extra time
or extra energy that I have

00:04:10.542 --> 00:04:12.000
really living, and making sure

00:04:12.083 --> 00:04:14.625
that I'm doing the things
which I think are important.

00:04:14.958 --> 00:04:18.083
Within healthcare these days,
there's an expectation,

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:20.542
both from healthcare professionals
and patients,

00:04:20.625 --> 00:04:23.500
that people can have a say
about what's important to them.

00:04:23.708 --> 00:04:25.875
However that can put
a lot of pressure on people

00:04:25.958 --> 00:04:29.542
to make sense of quite
a vast array of information

00:04:29.625 --> 00:04:31.875
and at a time when they actually
might want to defer

00:04:31.958 --> 00:04:33.417
the decision making to someone else.

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:37.167
If we have to, and you're not able
to tell us what you want,

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:41.083
we will do what we think
is best for you from what we know.

00:04:41.167 --> 00:04:43.833
It's not a yes, you've got a plan
or no, you haven't.

00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:47.583
It's more of a kind of evolution
of thinking.

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:51.667
And you can revisit them and change
them as your life is progressing

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.292
or as your values are changing.

00:04:53.375 --> 00:04:58.167
It's a true gift of love because
you're taking away that burden.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.000
Put your wishes down, file away,
and forget about it.

00:05:01.083 --> 00:05:04.000
Focus on the important bits of living
and getting on with life.

00:05:04.292 --> 00:05:07.750
We insure our house,
we insure our cars,

00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:09.417
we insure our pets.

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:12.583
Why are we not insuring ourselves?

00:05:12.667 --> 00:05:16.208
Nobody's going to say, "Well
Adil didn't say what he wanted".

00:05:16.292 --> 00:05:18.542
Because I did
and it's there on paper.

